{# Alert actions for every scheduled alert stanza below.

   Splunk `.conf` files have NO prefix-based stanza inheritance -- a stanza
   named `[Foo - bar]` inherits nothing from `[Foo]` (verified empirically
   against Splunk 10.2.0: a child stanza read back the global default
   severity and an empty cron). `[default]` is the only inheritance
   mechanism savedsearches.conf offers, and it is scoped to the app that
   declares it -- also measured: a `[default]` in a throwaway app reached
   its own searches and none of the 787 owned by the other 24 apps.

   Delivery is NOT a [default]. The app's metadata exports it to the system
   namespace (export = system), and an exported [default] reaches every
   other app's scheduled searches too, so each of their runs fires these
   actions. It is declared once here as the `alert_delivery` macro and
   rendered into each scheduled alert stanza with that stanza's name and
   severity; test_savedsearch_invariants.py fails on a scheduled alert
   stanza without it.

   TWO TIERS. The tier is the severity the stanza already declares, not a
   name list: severity >= splunk_docker_alert_page_min_severity (default 5)
   is critical and pages; everything below only triggers and is tracked, and
   is reported by [alert_digest_12h] at the bottom of this file. The macro
   collects the paging stanza names so the digest can exclude them by
   severity, however the rule files evolve. Turning
   splunk_docker_alert_two_tiers_enabled off restores the pre-tier render
   byte for byte (test_alert_two_tiers.py proves it).

   Every scheduled alert is delivered by one webhook to the ntfy alert hub,
   which fans out to Slack and Zammad. Splunk posts nowhere else. The URL
   is allowlisted in alert_actions.conf.j2, and assert_alerting_gates.yml
   fails the converge when it is not an https URL. Mirrors
   splunk_docker_saved_search_delivery_ntfy in 16-saved-search-overrides.yml
   so a search rendered here and one applied as a REST override to a search
   in another app get the same delivery shape.
#}
{% set critical_stanzas = namespace(names=[]) %}
{% macro alert_delivery(name, severity) %}
{% if splunk_docker_alert_two_tiers_enabled | default(true) %}
{% if (severity | int) >= (splunk_docker_alert_page_min_severity | default(5)) %}
{% set _ = critical_stanzas.names.append(name) %}
{% if splunk_docker_alert_ntfy_url %}
action.webhook = 1
action.webhook.param.url = {{ splunk_docker_alert_ntfy_url }}{{ splunk_docker_alert_ntfy_query }}
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{% elif splunk_docker_alert_ntfy_url %}
action.webhook = 1
action.webhook.param.url = {{ splunk_docker_alert_ntfy_url }}{{ splunk_docker_alert_ntfy_query }}
{% endif %}
{% endmacro %}
{# A paging stanza's own repeat budget is at least 24 hours: a critical
   alert pages at once and at most once per condition per day. Critical
   stanzas whose declared period is shorter are floored to 24h here (only
   when the two-tier switch is on); non-critical stanzas keep their own
   period. #}
{% macro alert_page_suppress_period(severity, period) %}
{% if splunk_docker_alert_two_tiers_enabled | default(true) and (severity | int) >= (splunk_docker_alert_page_min_severity | default(5)) %}
{% set p = period | string %}
{% set secs = (p[:-1] | int) * {'s': 1, 'm': 60, 'h': 3600, 'd': 86400}.get(p[-1:], 86400) %}
{% if secs < 86400 %}24h{% else %}{{ p }}{% endif %}
{% else %}
{{ period -}}
{% endif %}
{% endmacro %}
{# Informational events (no action needed) go to the hub's status topic, which
   has its own Slack channel and never opens a Zammad ticket. Same hub URL
   with the topic swapped and default priority. #}
{% set status_delivery %}
{% if splunk_docker_alert_ntfy_url %}
action.webhook = 1
action.webhook.param.url = {{ splunk_docker_alert_ntfy_url.rsplit('/', 1)[0] }}/status{{ splunk_docker_alert_ntfy_query | replace('priority=high', 'priority=default') }}
{% endif %}
{% endset %}

